                    <h1 class="content-title">Third-Party Risk <span class="gradient-text">Management</span></h1>
                    <p class="content-subtitle">
                        Manage vendor and third-party risk at scale — from initial due diligence and questionnaires 
                        to continuous monitoring. Understand how ServiceNow TPRM automates the entire vendor 
                        risk lifecycle using the Smart Assessment Engine.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <!-- What is TPRM -->
                <section class="content-section" id="what-is-tprm">
                    <h2>What is TPRM?</h2>
                    <p>
                        <strong>Third-Party Risk Management (TPRM)</strong> extends ServiceNow IRM beyond internal 
                        risk management to cover the entire ecosystem of external vendors, suppliers, partners, and 
                        service providers that your organization depends on. Every third-party relationship introduces 
                        risk — data breaches, compliance failures, supply chain disruptions, and operational dependencies.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        ServiceNow TPRM provides a structured, automated framework to assess, score, monitor, and 
                        manage vendor risk throughout the engagement lifecycle. It integrates directly with the broader 
                        IRM suite, meaning vendor risks roll up into your organization's overall risk posture alongside 
                        internal risks, controls, and compliance data.
                    </p>

                    <div class="info-card info-card--important">
                        <div class="info-card-title">Why TPRM Matters</div>
                        <p>
                            Regulatory frameworks like <strong>GDPR</strong>, <strong>SOC 2</strong>, <strong>DORA</strong>, 
                            and <strong>ISO 27001</strong> increasingly require organizations to demonstrate that they 
                            assess and monitor third-party risks. A vendor data breach can be as damaging as an internal 
                            one — and regulators hold <em>you</em> accountable for your vendors' security posture.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <!-- Vendor Risk Lifecycle -->
                <section class="content-section" id="vendor-lifecycle">
                    <h2>Vendor Risk Lifecycle</h2>
                    <p>
                        ServiceNow TPRM follows a structured lifecycle that takes a vendor engagement from initial 
                        request through active monitoring and eventual offboarding. Each stage has defined gates, 
                        approvals, and automation points.
                    </p>

                    <div class="diagram-block">
                        <div class="diagram-title">TPRM Vendor Engagement Lifecycle</div>
                        <div class="mermaid">
                        flowchart LR
                            A["1. Request<br/>Due Diligence"] --> B["2. Inherent Risk<br/>Assessment (IRQ)"]
                            B --> C["3. Due Diligence<br/>(External Assessment)"]
                            C --> D["4. Manager<br/>Review & Approval"]
                            D --> E["5. Contract Risk<br/>Approval"]
                            E --> F["6. Active<br/>Monitoring"]
                            F --> G["7. Offboarding<br/>& Close"]
                            
                            style A fill:transparent,stroke:#10b981,color:#d1d5db
                            style B fill:transparent,stroke:#3b82f6,color:#d1d5db
                            style C fill:transparent,stroke:#8b5cf6,color:#d1d5db
                            style D fill:transparent,stroke:#f59e0b,color:#d1d5db
                            style E fill:transparent,stroke:#ef4444,color:#d1d5db
                            style F fill:transparent,stroke:#06b6d4,color:#d1d5db
                            style G fill:transparent,stroke:#6b7280,color:#d1d5db
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <h3>Stage Breakdown</h3>
                    <div class="data-table-wrapper">
                        <table class="data-table">
                            <thead>
                                <tr>
                                    <th>Stage</th>
                                    <th>Who</th>
                                    <th>What Happens</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>1. Request Due Diligence</strong></td>
                                    <td>Business Owner / Procurement</td>
                                    <td>Internal user submits a request to engage a new third party. Basic vendor info is captured (company name, type, services provided).</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>2. Inherent Risk Assessment (IRQ)</strong></td>
                                    <td>Risk Team (Internal)</td>
                                    <td>An <strong>Internal Risk Questionnaire</strong> is completed to classify the vendor's inherent risk. Responses automatically generate a <strong>risk tier</strong> (Critical, High, Medium, Low) which determines the scope of due diligence required.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>3. Due Diligence</strong></td>
                                    <td>Third Party (External)</td>
                                    <td>Based on the risk tier, specific questionnaires and document requests are sent to the vendor. This may include SIG questionnaires, SOC 2 report requests, or custom assessment templates.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>4. Manager Review</strong></td>
                                    <td>Risk Manager</td>
                                    <td>Risk manager reviews assessment responses, evaluates findings, identifies gaps, and creates issues for any unacceptable findings.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>5. Contract Risk Approval</strong></td>
                                    <td>Senior Leadership / Legal</td>
                                    <td>Final risk-based approval for the engagement. Risk conditions and SLA requirements may be embedded into the contract.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>6. Active Monitoring</strong></td>
                                    <td>Automated / Risk Team</td>
                                    <td>Ongoing monitoring through periodic reassessments, risk intelligence feeds, and automated indicator checks. The system alerts teams when vendor risk posture changes.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>7. Offboarding</strong></td>
                                    <td>Procurement / IT</td>
                                    <td>When the engagement ends, the vendor is offboarded — access is revoked, data handling is verified, and the engagement is formally closed.</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <!-- Smart Assessment Engine -->
                <section class="content-section" id="assessment-engine">
                    <h2>Smart Assessment Engine (SAE)</h2>
                    <p>
                        The <strong>Smart Assessment Engine</strong> is the questionnaire and survey framework that 
                        powers TPRM assessments. It replaced the legacy "Classic Assessment" framework starting 
                        with the Zurich release and provides a modern, low-code approach to building and managing 
                        vendor assessments.
                    </p>

                    <h3>Key Capabilities</h3>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Template Designer</strong> — Drag-and-drop, low-code interface for building questionnaires. Supports branching logic, conditional questions, and section delegation.</li>
                        <li><strong>SIG Questionnaire Support</strong> — Out-of-the-box templates for the Standard Information Gathering (SIG) questionnaire, the industry-standard vendor assessment format.</li>
                        <li><strong>Automated Triggering</strong> — Assessments can be triggered automatically based on IRQ responses, risk tiers, recurring schedules, or event-driven workflows (e.g., a security incident at the vendor).</li>
                        <li><strong>Collaboration Features</strong> — Live presence indicators, section delegation (allowing different vendor teams to complete different sections), and question-level comments for seamless back-and-forth.</li>
                        <li><strong>Scoring & Evidence</strong> — Responses are auto-scored against defined criteria, and vendors can attach evidence documents directly to individual questions.</li>
                    </ul>

                    <div class="info-card info-card--tip">
                        <div class="info-card-title">Assessment Triggering Flow</div>
                        <p>
                            The most common pattern is: <strong>IRQ Score → Risk Tier → Assessment Template Selection</strong>. 
                            For example, a "Critical" tier vendor might receive a full 300-question SIG assessment, 
                            while a "Low" tier vendor gets a simplified 20-question security checklist. This is 
                            configured through <strong>Vendor Risk Scoring Rules</strong> on the 
                            <code>sn_vdr_risk_asmt_vendor_risk_scoring_rule</code> table.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <!-- Vendor Tiering & Risk Scoring -->
                <section class="content-section" id="tiering-scoring">
                    <h2>Vendor Tiering & Risk Scoring</h2>
                    <p>
                        Not all vendors carry the same risk. A cloud provider hosting your customer data is fundamentally 
                        different from an office supplies vendor. ServiceNow TPRM uses a <strong>tiered classification 
                        system</strong> to ensure that risk management effort is proportional to actual risk.
                    </p>

                    <h3>Risk Tier Classification</h3>
                    <div class="data-table-wrapper">
                        <table class="data-table">
                            <thead>
                                <tr>
                                    <th>Tier</th>
                                    <th>Criteria</th>
                                    <th>Assessment Depth</th>
                                    <th>Reassessment Frequency</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><span style="color:#ef4444;font-weight:600;">Critical</span></td>
                                    <td>Handles PII/PHI, has system access, single-source dependency</td>
                                    <td>Full SIG + SOC 2 + Pen Test Review</td>
                                    <td>Annually + Event-Triggered</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><span style="color:#f59e0b;font-weight:600;">High</span></td>
                                    <td>Accesses internal systems, processes sensitive data</td>
                                    <td>SIG Core + SOC 2 Review</td>
                                    <td>Annually</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><span style="color:#3b82f6;font-weight:600;">Medium</span></td>
                                    <td>Limited data access, standard business services</td>
                                    <td>Abbreviated Questionnaire</td>
                                    <td>Every 2 Years</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><span style="color:#10b981;font-weight:600;">Low</span></td>
                                    <td>No data access, no system integration, commodity services</td>
                                    <td>Self-Certification Only</td>
                                    <td>Every 3 Years</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>

                    <h3>Component-Based Scoring</h3>
                    <p>
                        TPRM uses a <strong>component-based scoring model</strong> where the overall vendor risk 
                        score is calculated from weighted components. Each component evaluates a specific risk domain 
                        (e.g., Information Security, Business Continuity, Privacy, Compliance).
                    </p>

                    <div class="diagram-block">
                        <div class="diagram-title">Vendor Risk Score Composition</div>
                        <div class="mermaid">
                        flowchart TB
                            A["Overall Vendor<br/>Risk Score"] --> B["Information Security<br/>(Weight: 35%)"]
                            A --> C["Data Privacy<br/>(Weight: 25%)"]
                            A --> D["Business Continuity<br/>(Weight: 20%)"]
                            A --> E["Regulatory Compliance<br/>(Weight: 15%)"]
                            A --> F["Financial Stability<br/>(Weight: 5%)"]
                            
                            style A fill:transparent,stroke:#8b5cf6,color:#d1d5db
                            style B fill:transparent,stroke:#ef4444,color:#d1d5db
                            style C fill:transparent,stroke:#f59e0b,color:#d1d5db
                            style D fill:transparent,stroke:#3b82f6,color:#d1d5db
                            style E fill:transparent,stroke:#10b981,color:#d1d5db
                            style F fill:transparent,stroke:#6b7280,color:#d1d5db
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <!-- TPRM Data Model -->
                <section class="content-section" id="tprm-data-model">
                    <h2>TPRM Data Model</h2>
                    <p>
                        TPRM extends the core IRM data model with vendor-specific tables. The central entity is 
                        <code>core_company</code> (the standard ServiceNow company table), which is linked to 
                        vendor engagements, assessments, and risk scores through a set of dedicated 
                        <code>sn_vdr_risk_asmt_*</code> tables.
                    </p>

                    <div class="data-table-wrapper">
                        <table class="data-table">
                            <thead>
                                <tr>
                                    <th>Table</th>
                                    <th>Label</th>
                                    <th>Purpose</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><code>core_company</code></td>
                                    <td>Company</td>
                                    <td>Base table for all third-party entities (vendors, suppliers, partners). Contains company name, industry, contact info.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><code>sn_vdr_risk_asmt_vendor_engagement</code></td>
                                    <td>Vendor Engagement</td>
                                    <td>Tracks individual engagements with a vendor. A single company can have multiple active engagements (e.g., different contracts/services).</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><code>sn_vdr_risk_asmt_vendor_risk_scoring_rule</code></td>
                                    <td>Vendor Risk Scoring Rule</td>
                                    <td>Defines the logic for calculating vendor risk scores — tier thresholds, component weights, and scoring criteria.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><code>sn_vdr_risk_asmt_component</code></td>
                                    <td>Assessment Component</td>
                                    <td>Configures the scoring dimensions (e.g., "Information Security", "Privacy") used in component-based risk scoring.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><code>sn_vdr_risk_asmt_component_criteria</code></td>
                                    <td>Component Criteria</td>
                                    <td>Defines the specific criteria within each scoring component (e.g., "Has SOC 2 Type II" within Information Security).</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><code>sn_grc_profile</code></td>
                                    <td>GRC Profile / Entity</td>
                                    <td>When a vendor is onboarded into IRM, a GRC profile/entity is created to link the vendor to risks, controls, and compliance activities.</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>

                    <div class="diagram-block">
                        <div class="diagram-title">TPRM Entity Relationship</div>
                        <div class="mermaid">
                        erDiagram
                            core_company ||--o{ sn_vdr_risk_asmt_vendor_engagement : "has engagements"
                            core_company ||--o| sn_grc_profile : "maps to entity"
                            sn_vdr_risk_asmt_vendor_engagement ||--o{ asmt_assessment_instance : "triggers assessments"
                            sn_vdr_risk_asmt_vendor_engagement }o--|| sn_vdr_risk_asmt_vendor_risk_scoring_rule : "scored by"
                            sn_vdr_risk_asmt_vendor_risk_scoring_rule ||--o{ sn_vdr_risk_asmt_component : "composed of"
                            sn_vdr_risk_asmt_component ||--o{ sn_vdr_risk_asmt_component_criteria : "evaluated by"
                            sn_grc_profile ||--o{ sn_risk_risk : "has risks"
                            sn_grc_profile ||--o{ sn_compliance_control : "has controls"
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <!-- Continuous Vendor Monitoring -->
                <section class="content-section" id="continuous-vendor-monitoring">
                    <h2>Continuous Vendor Monitoring</h2>
                    <p>
                        TPRM doesn't end after the initial assessment. Ongoing monitoring ensures that vendor risk 
                        posture is continuously tracked and that changes trigger appropriate responses.
                    </p>

                    <h3>Monitoring Mechanisms</h3>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Periodic Reassessments</strong> — Scheduled reassessments based on risk tier (Critical vendors annually, Low vendors every 3 years). The system automatically generates and sends assessment requests.</li>
                        <li><strong>Risk Intelligence Feeds</strong> — Integration with external risk intelligence providers (e.g., BitSight, SecurityScorecard) to get real-time cybersecurity ratings. Score changes can automatically trigger reassessment workflows.</li>
                        <li><strong>Event-Driven Triggers</strong> — Security incidents, regulatory changes, or M&A activity involving a vendor can trigger ad-hoc assessments outside the regular schedule.</li>
                        <li><strong>Contract Renewal Gates</strong> — Before a vendor contract is renewed, the system can enforce a risk reassessment gate to ensure the vendor still meets your risk requirements.</li>
                    </ul>

                    <div class="info-card info-card--warning">
                        <div class="info-card-title">Fourth-Party Risk</div>
                        <p>
                            A growing concern in TPRM is <strong>fourth-party risk</strong> — the risk introduced by 
                            your vendors' vendors. For example, if your cloud provider depends on a specific DNS service 
                            that goes down, your operations are affected even though you have no direct relationship with 
                            that DNS provider. ServiceNow's CMDB dependency mapping and operational resilience capabilities 
                            can help map these cascading dependencies.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <!-- Developer Patterns -->
                <section class="content-section" id="developer-tprm">
                    <h2>Developer Patterns for TPRM</h2>
                    <p>
                        As a developer working with TPRM, you'll commonly encounter these implementation patterns 
                        and customization scenarios.
                    </p>

                    <h3>Common Developer Tasks</h3>
                    <div class="data-table-wrapper">
                        <table class="data-table">
                            <thead>
                                <tr>
                                    <th>Task</th>
                                    <th>Approach</th>
                                    <th>Key Tables / APIs</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td>Custom vendor onboarding workflow</td>
                                    <td>Flow Designer with approval steps and conditional assessment triggers</td>
                                    <td><code>core_company</code>, <code>sn_vdr_risk_asmt_vendor_engagement</code></td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td>Integrate external risk scores (BitSight, etc.)</td>
                                    <td>IntegrationHub spoke or Scripted REST API to pull scores and update vendor records</td>
                                    <td>Custom fields on <code>core_company</code>, Scripted REST API</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td>Custom scoring rules</td>
                                    <td>Configure scoring components and criteria; use Script Includes for complex weighted calculations</td>
                                    <td><code>sn_vdr_risk_asmt_component</code>, <code>sn_vdr_risk_asmt_component_criteria</code></td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td>Automated reassessment scheduling</td>
                                    <td>Scheduled Flow or Script that queries vendors by tier and last assessment date</td>
                                    <td><code>sn_vdr_risk_asmt_vendor_engagement</code>, Flow Designer</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td>Vendor portal (external-facing)</td>
                                    <td>Service Portal pages for vendor self-service — complete assessments, upload documents, view status</td>
                                    <td>Service Portal, Assessment API</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>

                    <h3>Code Pattern: Query Vendors by Risk Tier</h3>
                    <div class="code-block">
                        <div class="code-header">
                            <span class="code-lang">JavaScript — Server-Side Script Include</span>
                            <button class="code-copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code to clipboard">Copy</button>
                        </div>
                        <div class="code-body">
                            <pre><code class="language-javascript">// Script Include: VendorRiskUtils
var VendorRiskUtils = Class.create();
VendorRiskUtils.prototype = {
    initialize: function() {},

    getCriticalVendorsDueForReassessment: function() {
        var oneYearAgo = new GlideDateTime();
        oneYearAgo.addYearsUTC(-1);

        var gr = new GlideRecord('sn_vdr_risk_asmt_vendor_engagement');
        gr.addQuery('risk_tier', 'critical');
        gr.addQuery('state', 'active');
        gr.addQuery('last_assessment_date', '<=', oneYearAgo);
        gr.query();

        var vendors = [];
        while (gr.next()) {
            vendors.push({
                engagementId: gr.getUniqueValue(),
                vendorName: gr.vendor.getDisplayValue(),
                lastAssessed: gr.getValue('last_assessment_date'),
                riskScore: gr.getValue('risk_score')
            });
        }
        return vendors;
    },

    type: 'VendorRiskUtils'
};</code></pre>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="info-card info-card--tip">
                        <div class="info-card-title">Best Practice</div>
                        <p>
                            When integrating external risk intelligence, use <strong>IntegrationHub</strong> spokes 
                            rather than custom REST calls in Business Rules. IntegrationHub provides retry logic, 
                            error handling, and audit trails out of the box, and it's significantly easier to 
                            maintain across platform upgrades.
                        </p>
                    </div>
